    "## Exercise 4: Refactoring Repetitive Logic\n",
    "**Objective:** Identify repetitive code and refactor it into a reusable function with error handling.\n",
    "\n",
    "The code below calculates the BMI (Body Mass Index) for three different patients. It is repetitive and lacks error handling. \n",
    "\n",
    "Refactor this by creating a `calculate_bmi(weight, height)` function that includes a `try/except` block to prevent `ZeroDivisionError` (if height is 0) or `TypeError` (if strings are passed)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# --- BAD CODE (Repetitive and fragile) ---\n",
    "w1, h1 = 70, 1.75\n",
    "# bmi1 = w1 / (h1 ** 2)\n",
    "# print(f\"Patient 1 BMI: {bmi1}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "w2, h2 = 85, 1.80\n",
    "# bmi2 = w2 / (h2 ** 2)\n",
    "# print(f\"Patient 2 BMI: {bmi2}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "w3, h3 = \"ninety\", 0  # This will crash the program!\n",
    "# bmi3 = w3 / (h3 ** 2)\n",
    "# print(f\"Patient 3 BMI: {bmi3}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- YOUR REFACTORED CODE BELOW ---\n",
    "def calculate_bmi(weight, height):\n",
    "    # TODO: Add try/except for TypeError and ZeroDivisionError\n",
